{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness to determine preference. Skewness refers to the distribution of outcomes relative to the mean: a left-skewed lottery has a greater probability mass on the favorable (higher) outcomes compared to unfavorable (lower) ones. In this case, Lottery A offers 2400 Euros with 100% probability — a perfectly symmetric, deterministic outcome with no variability. Lottery B offers 2500 Euros with 33% probability, 2400 Euros with 66% probability, and 0 Euros with 1%. The distribution of B has a higher probability (66%) on the 2400 Euro outcome, which is equal to A’s outcome, and a small 33% chance of a higher outcome (2500), and a very small 1% chance of 0. This suggests that B is slightly right-skewed (more mass on lower outcomes), because the majority of probability (66%) is on 2400, and only a small portion is on higher or lower outcomes. Thus, B is not left-skewed; in fact, it is more right-skewed than A. A is not skewed at all (deterministic). Therefore, the decision reason would prefer a left-skewed lottery, and since neither lottery is left-skewed (A is flat, B is slightly right-skewed), the reason would not favor either. However, the reason is defined to prefer the more left-skewed lottery — so if no lottery is left-skewed, the reason would not apply. But the key is whether the individual uses this skewness-based rule. The relevant information for the reason is the distribution of outcomes and probabilities — specifically, whether a greater portion of probability is assigned to favorable outcomes (higher values) than to unfavorable ones. In B, the 33% chance of 2500 is favorable, but 66% is on 2400 (same as A), and 1% on 0 (unfavorable). So, favorable outcomes (2500 and 2400) have 99% of probability, but 33% of that is on 2500, and 66% on 2400. The probability mass on favorable outcomes is high, but the 1% on 0 is very low. However, since 2400 is not favorable relative to A (which gives 2400 for sure), and 2500 is only 100 more, the skewness is not clearly left-skewed. In fact, the majority of probability is on 2400, which is not higher than A’s outcome. So, B is not left-skewed. Therefore, the decision reason does not apply in a way that would prefer either lottery — both are either flat or slightly right-skewed. Thus, the reason is not activated in this case.",
  "step_2": "The individual's verbal report states: 'In A mache ich definitiv Gewinn. Der höchste Gewinn in B ist nur 100 Euro höher als der Gewinn in A. Diese 100 Euro sind mir das Risiko nicht wert, gar keinen Gewinn zu machen. Die Wahrscheinlichkeiten in B sind für meine Entscheidung daher nicht ausschlaggebend.' Translation: 'In A I definitely make a profit. The highest gain in B is only 100 euros higher than in A. These 100 euros are not worth the risk of making no gain at all. The probabilities in B are therefore not decisive for my decision.' The individual considers the guaranteed 2400 euros in A versus the possibility of 2500 in B (with only 33% chance) and 0 in 1%. The individual emphasizes that the 100 euro gain in B is not worth the risk of getting nothing (0 euros). The individual explicitly dismisses the probabilities in B as not decisive. The deliberation focuses on the certainty of gain in A versus the risk of losing the entire gain in B. The individual does not mention anything about distribution, skewness, or the shape of the probability mass. The justification is based on risk aversion — avoiding the possibility of zero payoff for a small gain. The individual does not reference any evaluation of skewness or the distribution of outcomes relative to favorable vs. unfavorable outcomes.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason is based on skewness — specifically, preferring a lottery that has a greater portion of probability on favorable (higher) outcomes. In this case, Lottery A has a deterministic 2400 euro outcome — no skewness. Lottery B has a 66% chance of 2400 (same as A), 33% of 2500 (higher), and 1% of 0 (unfavorable). The favorable outcomes (2500 and 2400) have 99% of the probability, but the 66% on 2400 is not higher than A’s outcome, and the 33% on 2500 is small. Thus, the probability mass on favorable outcomes is not clearly greater than in A. In fact, the 1% chance of 0 makes the distribution slightly right-skewed (more mass on lower values). Therefore, neither lottery is left-skewed. The reason would not apply to either. The individual’s reasoning is entirely based on risk of loss (fear of getting nothing), not on skewness. The individual does not mention skewness, distribution, or any comparison of favorable vs. unfavorable probability mass. Therefore, the decision reason was not used. The individual’s justification is based on risk aversion and certainty, not on skewness evaluation.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates the skewness of the lotteries. Skewness refers to the distribution of probabilities across outcomes. A lottery is left-skewed if a greater portion of probability is assigned to favorable outcomes (positive outcomes) relative to unfavorable ones (zero or negative outcomes). In this case, both lotteries have a positive outcome (money) and a zero outcome. The favorable outcome is 2500 Euros in A and 2400 Euros in B. The probabilities are: A has 33% chance of 2500 Euros and 67% chance of 0; B has 34% chance of 2400 Euros and 66% chance of 0. To assess skewness, we compare the probability mass on the positive outcome versus the zero outcome. A higher probability on the positive outcome (i.e., lower probability on zero) indicates greater left-skewness. Here, A has 33% on 2500 and 67% on 0, while B has 34% on 2400 and 66% on 0. Thus, A has a lower probability of zero (67% vs. 66%) and a slightly higher probability on the positive outcome (33% vs. 34%). Therefore, A is more left-skewed than B. The decision reason prefers the more left-skewed lottery, which would be A. Hence, the decision reason is applicable to this problem and can be used to evaluate A vs. B.",
  "step_2": "The individual states that they chose Lottery A because 'one hundred euros more can be won' — referring to the difference in the amount won in the positive outcome (2500 vs. 2400). The individual notes that the probability differences (1%) are negligible and do not influence their decision. The deliberation process centers on the magnitude of the monetary gain in the favorable outcome and dismisses the probability differences as irrelevant. There is no mention of probabilities being compared in terms of distribution, skewness, or the shape of the outcome distribution. The justification is based solely on the absolute difference in the prize amount and the perceived irrelevance of the small probability difference. Thus, the individual's reasoning is based on expected value differences (or at least a focus on the prize amount) rather than on skewness of the outcome distribution.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason — the skewness of the lotteries — is not considered by the individual. The individual does not mention the distribution of probabilities, nor does the individual refer to the shape of the outcome (e.g., whether one lottery has a higher probability on the positive outcome relative to zero). Instead, the individual focuses only on the difference in prize amounts (100 Euros) and dismisses the probability difference. The decision reason requires evaluating skewness — specifically, whether one lottery has a greater concentration of probability on the favorable outcome — but this is absent from the verbal report. Therefore, the individual did not use the skewness-based decision reason. The justification is based on a different criterion (prize amount difference), not on the distributional skewness. Thus, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

