  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason — skewness — is not considered by the individual. The individual does not evaluate the distribution of probabilities across outcomes, nor does the individual mention the idea that a greater probability on favorable outcomes makes a lottery more left-skewed. In fact, the individual focuses exclusively on the payoff amount and dismisses the probabilities as too similar. The decision reason requires a comparison of skewness, which involves assessing whether the positive outcome has a greater probability than the zero outcome (i.e., left-skewness). In this case, both lotteries have a large probability on zero (80% and 75%), so both are right-skewed, and neither is left-skewed. Therefore, the reason does not apply. Since the individual neither considers skewness nor applies the rule that prefers the more left-skewed lottery, the decision reason was not used. The individual’s reasoning is based on payoff size, not distributional features like skewness. Thus, the decision reason was not used in the decision-making process.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
